    "content": "timing and publication requirements: AIAs should be published prior to launch, peer review findings (full or summary) should be published pre-production for higher impact systems, and internal compliance timelines were introduced to allow transition for existing systems. The OCIO offers departmental support and maintains the AIA tool. For scope and applicability guidance see TBS’s scope guide: Guide on the Scope of the Directive. [...] The Treasury Board of Canada Secretariat (TBS) Directive on Automated Decision-Making (launched 4 March 2019 and put into force 1 April 2019) establishes mandatory requirements for federal departments that use automated decision systems (ADS) to make or assist administrative decisions. It sets a proportional, impact-based approach organized around the Algorithmic Impact Assessment (AIA) that determines four impact levels (Level I to Level IV) and maps those to mitigation steps. The Directive’s [...] The Treasury Board of Canada Secretariat, through the Office of the Chief Information Officer (OCIO) of Canada, oversees administration and compliance. The OCIO maintains the AIA tool, publishes operational guidance on peer reviews and system scope, and conducts biennial policy evaluations. Assistant Deputy Ministers and departmental project sponsors are directly accountable for ensuring that mandatory risk assessments are completed, procedural fairness is preserved, and completed AIAs and peer"
